research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-ex-harmonic-series-diverges.log-4859-research/wave7-dispatch/orchestrator-A3-judgment.log-12154-- **Immediate consequences, used later.** From $m \le x < m + 1$ one reads off $0 \le x - m < 1$ and $0 < (m+1) - x \le 1$; and $\lfloor x \rfloor = x$ exactly when $x$ is an integer, since an integer $m$ satisfies $m \le m < m + 1$ and uniqueness does the rest. The translation identity $\lfloor x + p \rfloor = \lfloor x \rfloor + p$ for an integer $p$ follows the same way: adding $p$ to $m \le x < m+1$ gives $m + p \le x + p < (m + p) + 1$, and uniqueness identifies $m + p$ as the integer part of $x + p$.
