research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:599158:+    "thm-limit-comparison-test": {
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-599159-+      "citations": [
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-599160-+        {
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-599161-+          "fact": "L1",
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-599162-+          "source": "def-real-limit",
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-599163-+          "source_section": "Definition",
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-599164-+          "quote": "A sequence $(x_k)$ of reals **converges** to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ when for\nevery rational $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K$ with\n$|x_k - x| < \\hat\\varepsilon$ for all $k \\ge K$. It is **Cauchy** when for\nevery rational $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K$ with\n$|x_k - x_l| < \\hat\\varepsilon$ for all $k, l \\ge K$.",
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-599165-+          "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-599166-+            "2.1",
--
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605865-+          "source_section": "Definition",
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605866-+          "quote": "the finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. In particular $s_0 = 0$, the empty sum, and\n$s_{n+1} = s_n + a_n$ for every $n$, those being exactly the two recursion clauses\nthat define the finite sum. Note that $s_n$ is the sum of the $n$ terms\n$a_0, \\dots, a_{n-1}$, so the index $n$ counts terms rather than naming the last\none.",
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605867-+          "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605868-+            "1.3"
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605869-+          ]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605870-+        },
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605871-+        {
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605872-+          "fact": "L5",
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:605873:+          "source": "thm-limit-comparison-test",
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605874-+          "source_section": "Statement",
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605875-+          "quote": "1. if $(q_k)$ converges with $\\lim_k q_k = L$ for a real $L > 0$\n   ([[def-real-limit]]), then $\\sum a_k$ converges **if and only if** $\\sum b_k$\n   converges;\n2. if $(q_k)$ converges with $\\lim_k q_k = 0$, then convergence of $\\sum b_k$\n   implies convergence of $\\sum a_k$; equivalently, divergence of $\\sum a_k$\n   implies divergence of $\\sum b_k$;\n3. if $(q_k)$ diverges to $+\\infty$ ([[def-divergence-to-infinity]]), then\n   convergence of $\\sum a_k$ implies convergence of $\\sum b_k$; equivalently,\n   divergence of $\\sum b_k$ implies divergence of $\\sum a_k$.",
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605876-+          "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605877-+            "4.1"
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605878-+          ]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605879-+        }
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605880-+      ],
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605881-+      "derivations": [
--
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605995-+{"id":"lem-nth-term-test","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Theorem 4.9 states exactly that convergence of a real series forces its terms to tend to zero; its contrapositive is logically identical. The item's arbitrary-starting-index paragraph is only the library's shift convention. The proof follows Hunter's Cauchy-tail argument with local indexing.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605996-+{"id":"thm-series-cauchy-criterion","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Theorem 4.8 gives the same iff Cauchy block criterion. The item changes from inclusive partial sums indexed from 1 to exclusive partial sums indexed from 0 and writes the block as sum_{k=m+1}^n a_k; these are convention deltas only. Its proof is the sourced partial-sum Cauchy argument adapted to those indices.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605997-+{"id":"lem-series-linearity","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Linearity follows from the sourced definition of a series as a limit of partial sums and the standard algebra of limits. The item combines addition, scalar multiplication, and the nonzero-scalar iff/divergence form into one library-facing lemma. The proof is locally composed from finite-sum and limit laws.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605998-+{"id":"lem-absolute-convergence-implies-convergence","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf","https://math.rice.edu/~semmes/math331.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Proposition 4.17 and Semmes Proposition 3.7.13 state exactly that every absolutely convergent real (indeed complex) series converges. The proof follows the standard sourced comparison of each finite tail with the absolute-value tail, expressed through the local Cauchy criterion.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-605999-+{"id":"thm-geometric-series","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Example 4.2 gives the exact sum 1/(1-r) for |r|<1 and records divergence for r>=1 and oscillatory divergence for r<=-1, exactly the two regimes in the item. The proof uses the same finite geometric-sum identity and null-power limit with local dependencies made explicit.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-606000-+{"id":"lem-telescoping-series","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter §4.1 states that sum(a_n-a_{n+1}) has partial sums a_1-a_{n+1}; the item gives the identical result from index 0, adds the iff with convergence of b_k, the value of the sum, and the general-start shift. The proof is the standard telescoping calculation adapted to exclusive finite sums.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-606001-+{"id":"thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Proposition 4.6 states exactly that a nonnegative series converges iff its partial sums are bounded above and otherwise diverges to infinity. The item additionally identifies the sum with the supremum, an immediate monotone-convergence consequence. The proof follows that sourced monotone-partial-sum argument.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-606002-+{"id":"thm-direct-comparison-test","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Theorem 4.19 gives the standard comparison theorem, and the item's nonnegative eventual inequality is its positive-series specialization together with the contrapositive divergence form and tail invariance. The proof is the standard bounded-partial-sum comparison adapted to an eventual bound.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
