research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689333-+{"id":"lem-nth-term-test","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Theorem 4.9 states exactly that convergence of a real series forces its terms to tend to zero; its contrapositive is logically identical. The item's arbitrary-starting-index paragraph is only the library's shift convention. The proof follows Hunter's Cauchy-tail argument with local indexing.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689334-+{"id":"thm-series-cauchy-criterion","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Theorem 4.8 gives the same iff Cauchy block criterion. The item changes from inclusive partial sums indexed from 1 to exclusive partial sums indexed from 0 and writes the block as sum_{k=m+1}^n a_k; these are convention deltas only. Its proof is the sourced partial-sum Cauchy argument adapted to those indices.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689335-+{"id":"lem-series-linearity","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Linearity follows from the sourced definition of a series as a limit of partial sums and the standard algebra of limits. The item combines addition, scalar multiplication, and the nonzero-scalar iff/divergence form into one library-facing lemma. The proof is locally composed from finite-sum and limit laws.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689336-+{"id":"lem-absolute-convergence-implies-convergence","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf","https://math.rice.edu/~semmes/math331.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Proposition 4.17 and Semmes Proposition 3.7.13 state exactly that every absolutely convergent real (indeed complex) series converges. The proof follows the standard sourced comparison of each finite tail with the absolute-value tail, expressed through the local Cauchy criterion.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689337-+{"id":"thm-geometric-series","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Example 4.2 gives the exact sum 1/(1-r) for |r|<1 and records divergence for r>=1 and oscillatory divergence for r<=-1, exactly the two regimes in the item. The proof uses the same finite geometric-sum identity and null-power limit with local dependencies made explicit.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689338-+{"id":"lem-telescoping-series","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter §4.1 states that sum(a_n-a_{n+1}) has partial sums a_1-a_{n+1}; the item gives the identical result from index 0, adds the iff with convergence of b_k, the value of the sum, and the general-start shift. The proof is the standard telescoping calculation adapted to exclusive finite sums.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689339-+{"id":"thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Proposition 4.6 states exactly that a nonnegative series converges iff its partial sums are bounded above and otherwise diverges to infinity. The item additionally identifies the sum with the supremum, an immediate monotone-convergence consequence. The proof follows that sourced monotone-partial-sum argument.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689340-+{"id":"thm-direct-comparison-test","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Theorem 4.19 gives the standard comparison theorem, and the item's nonnegative eventual inequality is its positive-series specialization together with the contrapositive divergence form and tail invariance. The proof is the standard bounded-partial-sum comparison adapted to an eventual bound.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:689341:+{"id":"thm-limit-comparison-test","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://sites.und.edu/timothy.prescott/apex/web/apex.Ch9.S4.html","https://live.ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100b-real-analysis-spring-2025/mit18_100b_s25_lec_full.pdf"],"rationale":"The University of North Dakota theorem gives all three regimes: positive finite limit gives common behaviour, zero gives convergence only from the comparison series to the smaller one, and infinity gives divergence only from the comparison series to the larger one. The item uses real convergence versus divergence to +infinity explicitly, adds contrapositives, and shifts from index 1 to 0. Its proof follows the standard eventual two-sided inequalities.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689342-+{"id":"thm-cauchy-condensation","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.rice.edu/~semmes/math331.pdf"],"rationale":"Semmes Theorem 3.9.1 states exactly that a monotonically decreasing nonnegative family from 1 has a convergent series iff its condensed series sum 2^k a_{2^k} converges. The proof follows the sourced dyadic block bounds, with the local finite-sum and index conventions written out.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689343-+{"id":"thm-p-series-rational","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf","https://math.rice.edu/~semmes/math331.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Example 4.21 and Semmes's condensation chapter give the standard iff threshold p>1 for positive real exponents. The item restricts to rational p because that is the power operation available at this reading level and formalises canonical naturals, so it is a semantic specialization. The proof is the standard condensation-to-geometric-series argument.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689344-+{"id":"thm-root-test","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Theorem 4.27 states the limsup root test with absolute convergence below 1 and divergence above 1; the surrounding text gives both outcomes at equality. The item merely reindexes |a_n|^(1/n) as |a_{k+1}|^(1/(k+1)) to avoid exponent 1/0. Its proof follows the sourced geometric comparison.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689345-+{"id":"thm-ratio-test","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convergence_tests"],"rationale":"The source states the limsup/liminf ratio test exactly: limsup below 1 gives absolute convergence and liminf above 1 gives divergence, with the intervening regime inconclusive. The item makes nonvanishing and the index-shift convention explicit. Its proof is the standard eventual geometric comparison.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689346-+{"id":"cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.csudh.edu/~pong/a3/Notes_01.html"],"rationale":"The CSUDH notes give the full ratio-root liminf/limsup chain, state that the root test is strictly stronger, and use the same sequence 2^{(-1)^n-n} as witness. The item shifts indices and expands both convergence and divergence translations through local test statements. Its proof follows the sourced inequality-and-witness argument.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689347-+{"id":"thm-kummer-test","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf"],"rationale":"Thomson–Bruckner–Bruckner Theorem 3.37 gives exactly the positive-weight Kummer expression, the positive-liminf convergence criterion, and the eventual-nonpositive plus divergent reciprocal-weight criterion. The proof follows the sourced telescoping comparison, expanded with extended-real tail-infimum details.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689348-+{"id":"cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf"],"rationale":"Both textbooks explicitly identify d'Alembert's ratio test as Kummer's test with constant weights. The item states only the two limsup/liminf ratio conclusions recovered in the positive-term setting and proves them through reciprocal bounds, so it is an expanded semantic specialization.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-689349-+{"id":"cor-raabe-test","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources state Raabe's positive-term test and identify it as Kummer with harmonic weights. The item uses k+1 rather than k because its naturals start at zero, and uses liminf/limsup rather than assuming a single limit. The proof is the sourced Kummer specialization with that convention delta.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
